  "description": "The third and last arm of READ-USER-SEC-FILE's ordered EVALUATE WS-RESP-CD, WHEN OTHER at app/cbl/COUSR02C.cbl:346-352. No arrangement of seeded rows can produce it - it needs a genuine I/O failure - so the outcome is forced on the readForUpdate operation with RESP 19, which is DFHRESP(NOTOPEN). The arm DISPLAYs 'RESP:' WS-RESP-CD 'REAS:' WS-REAS-CD, sets the error flag, issues 'Unable to lookup User...' and moves -1 into FNAMEL rather than USRIDINL, so the cursor lands on the first name.\n\nThe DISPLAY line and the screen text travel on DIFFERENT channels and this case states both as such. The DISPLAY is a variable-width line - twenty-eight characters, from a five-character literal, a nine-digit S9(09) COMP field, another five-character literal and another nine-digit field - and belongs in expectedMessages. 'Unable to lookup User...' is screen text: it goes into WS-MESSAGE PIC X(80), is truncated to ERRMSGO PIC X(78) by the MOVE at :270, and belongs in the send's BMS payload. Mixing the two, as this folder previously did, makes a two-byte truncation defect invisible.",
